    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py`, and all tests pass (3 passed).\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(ticket_text(ticket))]\nreply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **Stop sequence:** `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]` is passed through `invoke`, the standard way every LangChain chat model accepts one, so it works with any model.\n- **Getting the reply text:** in the pinned `langchain-core==1.6.5`, a reply's `content` can be a plain string or a list of content blocks, depending on the provider. `reply.text` handles both cases. It returns a subclass of `str`, so I convert it with `str(...)` before `.strip()` to return a plain `str`.\n\nThe existing tests didn't cover this function, so I added `tests/test_summary.py`. It uses LangChain's built-in fake chat model with a reply made of content blocks. It checks that the function makes exactly one call, sends a system message then a user message with the right contents, passes `stop == [\"\\n\\n\"]`, and returns a stripped plain `str`. No dependencies were added."
